# Discussion

<Interpret. Lead with what reproduced and why it is robust (scale/seed-invariant, across
several read-outs). Then any negative result: state the mechanism/cause and its downstream
consequences (often one modelling fact explains several panel mismatches — say so rather
than listing them as independent failures). Then the reproduction-vs-replication framing
(below) and the accepted limitations.>

**Reproduction vs. replication.** In the NASEM terminology [@NASEM2019] *reproduction* re-runs
the same code on the same data; *replication* re-derives a paper's findings with **independent
methods and independently-sourced data** — which is what this is. Separate **mechanism-level**
targets (a sign / pattern / ordering that any reasonable input reproduces) from **decimal-level**
targets (a number needing the paper's exact input): the former reproduce; the latter are capped by
unavailable inputs and stated as limitations, never claimed as matches.

## Replication scorecard

Against the criteria in `original_study/analysis/targets.md`. **Status:** yes = reproduced ·
partial = realization-dependent/incomplete · out = out of scope. **Tier** (Phase 1.5):
*mech* = mechanism-level · *dec* = decimal-level (needs the paper's exact input).

| Criterion | Tier | Status | Note |
|---|:---:|:---:|---|
| <T1 criterion> | mech | **yes** | `{python} fmt(M.get('n_nodes'),0)` nodes … |
| <T2 criterion> | dec | **partial** | <mechanism holds, number caps on the substitute input> |
| <extended targets> | dec | **out** | <deferred / data gap / framework gap> |

